{"conversation":"Hmm, now that you say it, I think my code only checks neighbors of each node once, and doesn't go deeper to color neighbors of neighbors. So it might miss conflicts further in the graph. Maybe I need a way to explore the graph more fully, like using BFS or DFS to color nodes layer by layer.","python_code":"class Solution:
    def isBipartite(self, graph: List[List[int]]) -> bool:
        n = len(graph)
        colors = [-1] * n  # -1 means uncolored

        for i in range(n):
            if colors[i] == -1:  # not colored yet
                colors[i] = 0
                stack = [i]
                while stack:
                    node = stack.pop()
                    for neighbor in graph[node]:
                        if colors[neighbor] == -1:
                            colors[neighbor] = 1 - colors[node]
                            stack.append(neighbor)
                        elif colors[neighbor] == colors[node]:
                            return False
        return True
        "}

